{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1527926-ministry-s-discipline","id":1527926,"title":"Ministry's discipline","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Vampire: The Masquerade"],"posts":[{"date":"2024-01-04T09:19:26-08:00","id":1527926,"title":"Ministry's discipline","content":"Hi, guys! I'm wondering, what do you prefer: Serpentis or V5 Protean variant?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3792-trueann","name":"trueann","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3792_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"167"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-01-04T09:57:12-08:00","id":1527928,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>trueann</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1527926-ministry-s-discipline#post1527926\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Hi, guys! I'm wondering, what do you prefer: Serpentis or V5 Protean variant?</div> </div> </div> </div>Must admit, that while one can somewhat guess, i have no idea of what &quot;Ministry's Protean&quot; package is actually like - care to summarize it?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1147-baaldam","name":"Baaldam","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1147_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-01-04T10:23:19-08:00","id":1527930,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Baaldam</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1527926-ministry-s-discipline?p=1527928#post1527928\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Must admit, that while one can somewhat guess, i have no idea of what &quot;Ministry's Protean&quot; package is actually like - care to summarize it?</div> </div> </div> </div>In V5 Followers of Set is called Ministry and they have Protean instead of Serpentis \ud83d\udc0d<br /> <br /> The <a href=\"https://whitewolf.fandom.com/wiki/Ministry_(VTM)\" target=\"_blank\">Ministry</a> use Protean to take on the shape or characteristics of snakes, as they once did using <a href=\"https://whitewolf.fandom.com/wiki/Serpentis\" target=\"_blank\">Serpentis</a>. <br /> <br /> <a href=\"https://whitewolf.fandom.com/wiki/Ministry_(VTM)?so=search\" target=\"_blank\">https://whitewolf.fandom.com/wiki/Mi...VTM)?so=search</a>","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3792-trueann","name":"trueann","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3792_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"167"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-01-04T11:07:01-08:00","id":1527934,"title":"","content":"VTR2e protean. For a linear discipline, it\u2019s very flexible: three partial body changes of your choice at Protean 2, up to 3 animal forms at 3 and a 4th supernatural feature at 4.<br /> <br /> I very much prefer the principle of giving Protean rather than snake protean+whatever other powers Setites should have. But after VtR, V5 Protean is disappointing for any clan.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":7}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3523-spencer-from-the-hills","name":"Spencer from The Hills","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"546"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-01-04T11:17:20-08:00","id":1527936,"title":"","content":"The V5 wiki has a <a href=\"https://vtm.paradoxwikis.com/Protean\" target=\"_blank\">list</a> of Protean powers with brief summaries. Basically, Setite/Minister's Protean roster is likely to be pretty similar to a Gangrel's; they're both going to go for Feral Weapons, for example, but the Minister who clings to the snake theme would probably go for snake fangs or a lashing tail rather than claws (it isn't actually limited to fangs vs claws in the full description); thematically, a Gangrel's probably more likely to get Earth Meld than a Minister, but Shapechange suits the themes of both rather well and the only likely difference between them is a preference for snakes, jackals, and maybe other Egyptian-themed animals (assuming they don't eschew the Set stuff altogether). This is somewhat tangential, but I question how often they'd actually go for the Eyes of the Serpent amalgam via presence given how easily vampires can resist and how much more broadly useful Awe/Dread are.<br /> <br /> I'm personally torn on the answer to trueann's question. I don't particularly care for Serpentis in previous editions given how frequently it lapses into snake-themed Protean, but if we're talking about the RAW V5 approach to Disciplines, I'd have to say that I find Ministry Protean to be a little bit worse because of the one power per Discipline dot restriction (and the Amalgam setup for Heart of Darkness which requires two dots of an out-of-clan Discipline) paired with the lack of non-Vicissitude Protean options for many levels. This makes things pretty samey across the board, even more than it was before with Serpentis.<br /> <br /> Though trueann didn't bring it up, the choice to make the Chimerstry powers Obfuscate + Presence amalgams also means that Ministers can access them as easily as any Ravnos (who swapped Fortitude for Presence in this version). Probably not the best decision as far as drawing clear distinctions as far as clan identities and abilities go (and I think there's a decent rationale for making them Obfuscate + Fortitude amalgams anyway - with Fortitude serving to 'solidify' and externalize the normally internal perception-tampering that Obfuscate involves), but temptation through illusion is such a common trope that I'm not as cross about that as I might otherwise be.<br /> <br /> Edit: If we're stepping outside WOD, then like Spencer from The Hills, I'd say that VTR2e's Protean does it best.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":27894,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27894-paramecius","user":"Paramecius","date":". 01-04-2024, 02:19 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":5}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27894-paramecius","name":"Paramecius","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27894_1.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2023"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"29"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-01-04T11:40:16-08:00","id":1527939,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Paramecius</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1527926-ministry-s-discipline?p=1527936#post1527936\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I'm personally torn on the answer to trueann's question. I don't particularly care for Serpentis in previous editions given how frequently it lapses into snake-themed Protean,</div> </div> </div> </div>Indeed<br /> Luckly DA V20 a bit mitigated that<br /> Insteas The Eyes of the Serpent you can have <b>Enchanting Gaze</b> (This power makes the vampire the proverbial flame to a moth. <i>She takes on an alluring, enchanting feature</i>, such as a serpent\u2019s gold eyes or a pearlescent sheen to her skin, etc)<br /> No more The Tongue of the Asp, have a nice\u200b <b>Typhonic Maw</b> (The vampire\u2019s jaw becomes a ferocious, malleable thing she can control in a variety of ways.)<br /> Okay, Skin of the Adder and <b>Serpent\u2019s Flesh\u200b </b>is tecnically the same, but hey, in The Vampire Players Guide Second Edition we had <b>Mummify </b>a nice and so so useless power, but fun one!<br /> The Form of the Cobra? Don't make me laugh! The big boys play with <b>Typhonic Avatar</b> (Now, the Setite can become a Typhonic Beast, a creature of legend.)<br /> The Heart of Darkness and <b>Cheat the Scale of Anubis</b> from Dark Ages: Vampire Rulebook is functionally the same (with an added benefit, not only remove your heart, making you immune to staking, but you could remove all your organs and become nearly indestructible) but Mother of Monsters is nice too<br /> The important, we moved away the snake themed Thulsa Doom cosplayers and can have a Sutekh-themed Serpentis!<br /> <br /> As for the question, V5 Protean is weak conpared to seprentis<br /> \u200b<br /> <br /> \u200b","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83e\udd70","count":3},{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23890-shadeprowler","name":"Shadeprowler","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23890_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"928"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-01-04T14:49:22-08:00","id":1527956,"title":"","content":"I'll third Paramecius.<br /> <br /> Serpentis was always one of the Disciplines that felt entirely superfluous with Protean already existing, and it played too much into Thusla Doom from Conan instead of anything to do with Egyptian symbolism.<br /> <br /> But the way V5 handles combining Disciplines has a lot of off-putting side effects (esp. with any attempt at continuity between editions).<br /> <br /> So I prefer Serpentis because I prefer the older approach to Disciplines, even if Serpentis is far from what I would consider a well executed example of the older style.<br /> <br /> VtR 2e's approach I don't think really covers Serpentis that well, but it's definitely something that makes V5 Protean look bad in terms of how you can make it a more customizable and variable power that could appeal to differently themed characters. That said, VtR 2e + Devotions (VtR's equivalent to Amalgams) would be more than capable of filling in the gaps, and can even tie into VtR's Bloodline systems which would already be appropriate for something like the Ministry if you were replicating them using VtR rules.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-01-04T15:35:43-08:00","id":1527959,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Shadeprowler</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1527926-ministry-s-discipline?p=1527939#post1527939\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Indeed<br /> Luckly DA V20 a bit mitigated that<br /> Insteas The Eyes of the Serpent you can have <b>Enchanting Gaze</b> (This power makes the vampire the proverbial flame to a moth. <i>She takes on an alluring, enchanting feature</i>, such as a serpent\u2019s gold eyes or a pearlescent sheen to her skin, etc)<br /> No more The Tongue of the Asp, have a nice <b>Typhonic Maw</b> (The vampire\u2019s jaw becomes a ferocious, malleable thing she can control in a variety of ways.)<br /> Okay, Skin of the Adder and <b>Serpent\u2019s Flesh\u200b </b>is tecnically the same, but hey, in The Vampire Players Guide Second Edition we had <b>Mummify </b>a nice and so so useless power, but fun one!<br /> The Form of the Cobra? Don't make me laugh! The big boys play with <b>Typhonic Avatar</b> (Now, the Setite can become a Typhonic Beast, a creature of legend.)<br /> The Heart of Darkness and <b>Cheat the Scale of Anubis</b> from Dark Ages: Vampire Rulebook is functionally the same (with an added benefit, not only remove your heart, making you immune to staking, but you could remove all your organs and become nearly indestructible) but Mother of Monsters is nice too<br /> The important, we moved away the snake themed Thulsa Doom cosplayers and can have a Sutekh-themed Serpentis!<br /> <br /> As for the question, V5 Protean is weak conpared to seprentis<br /> \u200b<br /> <br /> \u200b</div> </div> </div> </div>I can't judge V5 Protean because I haven't read the manual yet, but I totally love VDA20 Serpentis and find it hard to think of playing without, even Masquerade. It has a proper theme and it makes sense. Its resemblance with Protean is only superficial. Protean is about shapeshifting and melting in the earth - it gives me very lupinesque vibes and pushes my headcanon towards a link between Ennoia and the Garou. VDA20 Serpentis is about manifesting the gifts of Typhon, Echidna, Sutekh, Apep. If one likes mythology, the possibilities are incredible.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/16965-hermeticus","name":"Hermeticus","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"431"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-01-04T20:24:39-08:00","id":1527971,"title":"","content":"After getting hands-on with the material and seeing how it all fits together, it's very much a change for the better and I like it.<br /> <br /> First, we have to confront that in past editions, Setites have been Uncomfortable Cultural Baggage with a neat candy shell of &quot;not racist at all, you guys&quot; pulp fantasy tropes. Yeah, later editions worked around it by tying it to <i>actual</i> Egyptian mythology as opposed to ridiculous Thulsa Doom/Mola Ram shenanigans, and treating that material with a &quot;fair, for its time&quot; level of respect. But at the end of the day, that was still the Setites' hat and there was no way around it without a complete overhaul of the clan. And, that's what we got, by and large -- with the added bonus of orthodox Setites still being around, should one be necessary as a story antagonist.<br /> <br /> And, that brings me to my second point: Setites were inherently antagonistic. If not outright so through path of enlightenment or blood sorcery, their discipline combinations -- Akhu included -- encouraged lone wolfy, scene-stealer type play, which tended to exclude other players while scenes were being run specifically for that character. That got fixed, too.<br /> <br /> What we got in v5, was much like Malkavians and Ravnos, the flavor and core powers of their proprietary disciplines were added to other, more appropriate and broadly available, ones. Eyes of the Serpent and Lingering Kiss -- which you'll note, stands in for at least one general blood sorcery ritual and merit which plays quite well for Setites, and one Setite-specific merit -- are in Presence. Nor do I believe it's by accident Setites can access the Chimerstry sub-discipline completely in-clan; as already mentioned, illusions and hallucinations are common temptation and seduction tropes.<br /> <br /> Now, as for having to &quot;give up&quot; awe or daunt for eyes of the serpent...if you buy a new discipline power, you do not have to take a power of that level. Presence 2 (and 4) are noteworthy for that, being every presence power scales to discipline level, and those levels are somewhat of a &quot;dead zone&quot; of supplementary powers built around a clear expectation players may well want to buy lower-level powers without having felt like they've missed out. Fortitude is the same way, given how nice level 1 and 3 powers are, while the only powerful or non-situational level 2 or 4 power is toughness.<br /> <br /> As for protean...<br /> <br /> You have no need for tongue of the asp's &quot;darkvision&quot;, being you can get eyes of the beast and that power is a soft prerequisite for eyes of the serpent. Feral weapons perfectly replicates tongue and typhonic maw, consistent with v5's overall nerfing of damage and aggravated sources. Losing mummify is tough, but it's a power deprecated long before v5, and only useful situationally.<br /> <br /> Skin of the adder is likewise obsolete, considering soak doesn't really exist in v5 and that damage and aggravated sources were nerfed -- and being Fortitude 2 is a prerequisite for heart of darkness, you're getting the v5 analog to skin of the adder by taking resilience and toughness. Unfettered heart is in practice just as useful as heart of darkness, without the added vulnerability of having a meat-phylactery.<br /> <br /> Yes, fortitude is out-of-clan for Setites and that's a pain in the butt. But, one of the best predator types -- which just so happens to perfectly fit in with Setites' whole shtick and plays to their strengths -- gives a free dot of fortitude. Picking a predator type that's a no-brainer for a setite, puts you 14 whopping experience points away from meeting the prerequisite for what is now arguably the weakest protean 5 power.<br /> <br /> As for form of the cobra and/or typhonic avatar, once again for those in the back, v5 nerfed damage and aggravated sources. And part and parcel of that, as well as the overall setting nerf, is getting rid of war forms. The last one in the game is zulo form, and that got hit hard with the nerf bat. Shapechange and metamorphosis are still there, you can just no longer expect to take alternate forms, and suddenly punch in the heavyweight class despite dumping physical attributes and skills.<br /> <br /> So, in exchange for not really losing anything (provided a player builds competently), setites get in-clan access to earth meld and mist form. Otherwise known as two of the most powerful survival discipline powers in the whole-ass game.<br /> <br /> In v5, especially when it comes to clans and bloodlines which used to have proprietary disciplines, you can't look at one discipline (or the lack thereof) in a vacuum. You have to look at the entire package, amalgams included, to see how it fits together. You can't just look at protean and think, &quot;aww they don't get serpentis anymore&quot;; you have to look at obfuscate, presence, <i>and</i> protean to notice Setites don't <i>just</i> get serpentis, they get <i>so much more</i>.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4e","count":5},{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/431-theodrim","name":"Theodrim","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar431_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1841"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-01-04T23:45:11-08:00","id":1527987,"title":"","content":"@Theodrim: I don't 100% disagree with you in regards to the alternatives and replacements that are available through V5. In particular, Tongue of the Asp's prior tremorsense/darkvision utility can be functionally replaced with Eyes of the Beast; hell, it probably wouldn't be much of an ask to get most STs to let you <i>call</i> it Tongue of the Asp if you want to keep the tasting/sensing vibrations with a forked tongue flavor - a darting forked tongue's possibly a less obvious masquerade breach than glowing red eyes under various circumstances but it's also likely to invite more wariness from Kindred who'd associate with the Ministry. I also agree that you can partially cover Skin of the Adder's ground for free via Resilience with certain Predator Types. Siren's hardly a stretch for most Ministers, and even Consensualist can work well for Serpents who particularly take to the notion of corruption of liberation/giving people enough rope to hang themselves.<br /> <br /> Still, if we're talking about RAW V5, 14 experience is 14 sessions, plus Vitae from a vampire who has the power you want, with all the usual risks that entails, and any boons that you have to trade in or concede to others along the way. That's a pretty rocky road to get something that was originally straightforwardly available to them. I do agree that Toughness would probably be the best thing a Minister gets out of angling for Heart of Darkness anyway since it does kind of suck, but I don't see the crappiness of the power itself as a point in favor of the Ministry's V5 arrangement when it was at least less work to get in previous editions. The Unfettered Heart is indeed a less costly alternative, but it's more resistance than immunity to staking; not actually quite as useful for that particular niche even if it's probably the less painful investment for someone that wouldn't otherwise be going for Fortitude.<br /> <br /> It's also true that a Minister can circle back for Eyes of the Serpent or Awe/Daunt at later levels in Presence, but I'm less convinced that makes a particularly compelling argument in favor of the V5 setup; deliberately making powers relatively lame/sparse at particular levels so that people feel less bad about taking a level one power later on doesn't change the fact that their cost is doubling at least, and those who already have those powers are still left with an assortment of alternatives that can be comfortably replaced with lower level abilities that would normally cost half as much (which seems kind of underwhelming if not outright problematic for balance). Granted, they could commit a free dot from a Predator Type to it, but that means they're probably sacrificing Lingering Kiss for it, which would otherwise fit quite well into the whole snaky tempter schtick for someone who wants to go all-in on it unless they're willing to kick it a bit further down the road as a substitute for Dread Grace, Entrancement, or True Love's Face, and that's a big ask mechanically and thematically. There's nothing wrong with hard choices per se, but this one's only hard if a player's going for a particular flavor. From a practical perspective, there are clear winners and losers here and the winners can still get up to pretty similar tricks.<br /> <br /> And that's the crux of my issue with the V5 shifts for the Ministry: there are technically ways to purchase all their formerly proprietary stuff but it tends to mean going out of your way and shooting yourself in the foot en route as you deliberately ignore obviously superior competing options that are more generic (Misty Form vs Heart of Darkness/The Unfettered Heart being another prominent example). In practice, this likely means that there's less to distinguish Ministry player characters from Gangrels or other Protean users than before when Serpentis was its own thing, given that they had Obfuscate and Presence in prior editions too (and there's a grand total of one Protean amalgam power associated with either of these Disciplines in this edition). Even so, I still only count the situation as <i>slightly</i> worse (in the sense of being blander) since, from my perspective, Serpentis was mostly unremarkable and redundant to begin with. Opening up Chimerstry to the Ministry is a wash as far as I'm concerned since it's both a good and bad thing: it's cool and fitting for Ministers, but I can't really ignore how it hoses the Ravnos in the process compared to, say, the Malkavians or the Ventrue, since other clans have at least work a bit harder to ape their particular tricks.<br /> <br /> Stepping away from RAW, though, I think there are some pretty easy fixes. Additional Protean powers, amalgams and otherwise, are easy to come up with and would enrich the tapestry quite a bit: making Eyes of the Serpent a level one Protean power with a Presence 1 prerequisite is about the most low-effort change imaginable, and a scaling level one Protean power - Sinuousness or whatever - that provides bonuses relating to flexibility (say vs grapples/mundane physical constraints and maybe an optional Rouse Check to squeeze into or out of tight spots ala the Rathead gift from prior editions of WTA) wouldn't take much more trouble to write out and it would also be a good fit for Tzimisce. Overall, I <i>like</i> that V5 wanted to expand the functional and thematic scope of Disciplines, I just think it leaves a bit to be desired in practice. Stepping away from powers themselves, a Horned Vipers/Warrior Setites Loresheet could potentially serve as a way to ease the costs for someone who's hellbent on Heart of Darkness or just wants a cleaner route to Fortitude/Potence. There isn't really a shortage of options within the edition for people who regard the situation as needing correcting.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27894-paramecius","name":"Paramecius","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27894_1.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2023"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"29"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-01-05T04:31:20-08:00","id":1528008,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Paramecius</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1527926-ministry-s-discipline?p=1527987#post1527987\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">@Theodrim: I don't 100% disagree with you in regards to the alternatives and replacements...</div> </div> </div> </div>A character only needs to taste the blood of another vampire to learn the first dot of an out-of-clan discipline; once they have the first dot, it can be raised on its own. Hence, why predator type is such a powerful tool in character generation; it provides discipline access a character may not otherwise have (or realistically get, during play). A v5 setite who starts with fortitude as their bonus power through predator type, never has to taste the blood of a vampire with fortitude because they already have it.<br /> <br /> As far as skin of the adder versus v5 fortitude, to get to my conclusion we need to ask why skin of the adder was so good in the first place. Simply put, it allowed the most common and dangerous aggravated sources to be soaked with stamina at reduced difficulty; meaning, more dice pool thanks to being able to blood buff stamina, and more soak successes thanks to reduced difficulty. That was necessary, due to how blown out of proportion damage pools were, and how abusable action economy was, in past editions. Defensive powers had to be gonzo, just to make them worth taking or using in the first place.<br /> <br /> Nerfing damage pools, getting rid of action economy abuse, changing bashing and lethal to halved/unhalved superficial, and restricting sources of aggravated damage eliminated the root cause of the issue. Absent that root cause, gonzo defensive powers aren't necessary and that allows fortitude to shine. Toughness getting special mention, as it works against superficial Willpower damage as well as being the only source of reducing unhalved damage available.<br /> <br /> Powers having &quot;dead zones&quot; isn't the sign of crappy or lame powers, or poor design. One or more levels just happen to give a wealth of good options, and the level after it is based around supplementary or circumstantial powers to give players the ability to buy multiple powers of a previous level. When you raise a discipline like presence or fortitude you're not just buying a new power, you're buying synergistic increases to the powers you've already bought. Opportunity cost and careful power selection to build a theme or concept, is just the name of the game in v5: every character has to deal with it, and no individual character can be everything at once.<br /> <br /> And yeah, that means certain clans can cop other clans' hats. Let's be honest with ourselves, most proprietary disciplines were either not very good, laughably overpowered, barely had enough content to justify being a discipline in its own right, or were protean reskins (those groups not being mutually exclusive). Folding those powers into common disciplines is a <i>good</i> thing; yeah, you can play a setite with unfettered access to the chimerstry line, but ravnos can now play stealthy, social, or spymaster/information broker in turn. Nosferatu can play social, (ever stronger than before) combat monster, and &quot;minion lord&quot; (and have those minions matter). And so on.<br /> <br /> Players can still go whole hog on a concept, and yeah that has an opportunity cost. If, for example, I'm playing malkavian and take a predator type with presence, I'm 54 experience away from playing &quot;OG dementation master&quot;. Start with sense the unseen, premonition, cloak of shadows, and daunt; then, buy chimerstry, dementation, melpominee, and fata morgana in that order. That's everything Dementation 1-5 did, crossing the line into OG dementation 6-7, but more broadly applicable while delivering greater utility on an individual level than any OG dementation power. All while still having room to grow through in-clan disciplines and presence alone.<br /> <br /> The price I pay for that is giving up the traditional discipline spreads for obfuscate, presence, and dominate, but that's just the name of the game. I can't do everything at once.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4e","count":3},{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/431-theodrim","name":"Theodrim","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar431_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1841"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-01-05T09:48:35-08:00","id":1528033,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Theodrim</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1527926-ministry-s-discipline?p=1528008#post1528008\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">A character only needs to taste the blood of another vampire to learn the first dot of an out-of-clan discipline; once they have the first dot, it can be raised on its own. Hence, why predator type is such a powerful tool in character generation; it provides discipline access a character may not otherwise have (or realistically get, during play). A v5 setite who starts with fortitude as their bonus power through predator type, never has to taste the blood of a vampire with fortitude because they already have it.</div> </div> </div> </div>Technically, a predator type grants a dot in a Discipline without the experience cost but the description of them doesn't say anything about alleviating the need for Vitae from other vampires to learn an out-of-clan Discipline in the first place; any complications arising therefrom may still apply depending on the Storyteller.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Powers having &quot;dead zones&quot; isn't the sign of crappy or lame powers, or poor design. </div> </div> </div>It doesn't have to be, but as you've noted before, context matters. The context here is still a system that defaults to incremental experience gains paired with dramatically increasing experience costs for Discipline powers. The potential synergies from lower-level powers themselves don't change whether you spend 5/7, 10/14, 15/21, 20/28, or even 25/35 experience to get them, and their scaling benefits remain tied to progression in the Discipline itself, which again obviously incentivizes getting them while they're cheap since you still get the synergies and scaling plus access to higher level powers in the deal. If later powers have to be made fairly minor/niche to take the sting out of these rapidly rising costs when one does circle back for earlier powers, then that calls their value into question. Something's gotta give here either way.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Folding those powers into common disciplines is a <i>good</i> thing; yeah, you can play a setite with unfettered access to the chimerstry line(...) </div> </div> </div>Folding powers into a tighter set of common Disciplines is good when it reduces the needless proliferation of functionally and conceptually redundant power sets, not simply as a matter of course and especially not when it doles out access to one of the key features of one clan to another at no cost whatsoever. Even so, I do want to emphasize that the sticking point for me here is <i>unfettered</i> access. V5 started with the implicit design goal of allowing somewhat open access to signature clan powers, but with increased costs for outsiders: others can get Dementation, but not as easily as Malkavians, while the same is true for Irresistible Voice in relation to the Ventrue and Vicissitude re: the Tzimisce. It's sloppy, inconsistent, and I'd say problematic when the system undercuts this goal, whether in the case of the Ministry vs the Ravnos, the Hecata vs the Salubri, or anyone else. I brought it up here about the Ministry in particular because this is a thread about them, not because I ignore it elsewhere.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Players can still go whole hog on a concept, and yeah that has an opportunity cost. If, for example, I'm playing malkavian and take a predator type with presence, I'm 54 experience away from playing &quot;OG dementation master&quot;. Start with sense the unseen, premonition, cloak of shadows, and daunt; then, buy chimerstry, dementation, melpominee, and fata morgana in that order. </div> </div> </div>In this case, however, all but Daunt are freely available at in-clan costs out of the box and Daunt itself is free in this build. Chimerstry and Fata Morgana aren't necessary for Dementation 1-5 at all: the Dementation amalgam power itself already covers the encroaching delusions and loss of control aspects of the Discipline. I have to go off the summary since what I've heard about the Player's Guide failed to justify its purchase for me, but Melpominee is more or less redundant itself since only specific Presence powers like Dread Gaze can't function through the use of one's voice anyway. The situation's obviously worse for a Minister chasing after the classic Serpentis in terms of opportunity and experience costs; you've more or less conceded this already in relation to Skin of the Adder and Heart of Darkness, albeit with the proviso that Skin of the Adder doesn't and shouldn't matter anymore in this edition.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> The price I pay for that is giving up the traditional discipline spreads for obfuscate, presence, and dominate, but that's just the name of the game. I can't do everything at once. </div> </div> </div>I hope we can at least agree that there's a vast, vast gulf between &quot;doing everything at once&quot; and &quot;doing one thing per Discipline level ever&quot;, but even if not, surely you can see how the latter setup strongly encourages pragmatic Discipline power selections over thematic ones, including selections based on clan themes.<br /> <br /> I still don't have a problem with making choices in and of itself. However, that doesn't mean that I have to accept all choice setups as desirable or fine as-is, and I don't accept that the choice between neat/evocative powers (if one happens to find the various Serpentis ports to be so) and optimal powers has to be as skewed as it is here.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27894-paramecius","name":"Paramecius","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27894_1.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2023"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"29"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-01-05T13:30:33-08:00","id":1528054,"title":"","content":"I've never been a fan of the followers of Set, like so many here Serpentis just struck me as &quot;the bad protean&quot;. But as I dived into VtDA, I'll admit, I was impressed as hell, it's just sad it took nearly 20 years for Serpentis to step out of Protean's shadow only to get melded into Protean a couple years later. <br /> <br /> So I guess I would rank them: <br /> <br /> VtR 2e Protean &gt; VtDA 20 Serpentis &gt; V5 Protean / Serpentis Combo &gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; Serpentis from any other edition.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27618-shadowcatx","name":"ShadowcatX","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27618_2.jpg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2022"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"560"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-01-05T18:26:55-08:00","id":1528073,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Paramecius</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1527926-ministry-s-discipline?p=1528033#post1528033\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Technically, a predator type grants a dot in a Discipline without the experience cost but the description of them doesn't say anything about alleviating the need for Vitae from other vampires to learn an out-of-clan Discipline in the first place; any complications arising therefrom may still apply depending on the Storyteller.</div> </div> </div> </div>It really doesn't depend on the storyteller; the core book is quite explicit. Page 244,<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> To later learn a completely new Discipline that is not one of their Clan Disciplines, the character also needs to taste the Blood of someone who possesses it. </div> </div> </div>It's not a &quot;completely new&quot; discipline, if the player already knows it. This is what the language means: to learn a completely new discipline, is to buy the first dot.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> The context here is still a system that defaults to incremental experience gains paired with dramatically increasing experience costs for Discipline powers. The potential synergies from lower-level powers themselves don't change whether you spend 5/7, 10/14, 15/21, 20/28, or even 25/35 experience to get them, and their scaling benefits remain tied to progression in the Discipline itself, which again obviously incentivizes getting them while they're cheap since you still get the synergies and scaling plus access to higher level powers in the deal. If later powers have to be made fairly minor/niche to take the sting out of these rapidly rising costs when one does circle back for earlier powers, then that calls their value into question. Something's gotta give here either way. </div> </div> </div>What you're missing is when you increase a discipline level, you're not just getting a new power. The powers you already have now function at the new level. The order in which you buy them doesn't matter, save for prerequisites.<br /> <br /> If you already have awe from presence 1, and buy daunt leveling presence to 2, you're not just getting daunt. You're getting daunt as it functions at level 2 (+2 dice to intimidation), and awe now functions at level 2 (+2 to relevant Charisma-related rolls). Or, if you bought resilience at fortitude 1 and toughness at level 2, and then buy fortify the inner facade with fortitude 3, you're not just getting fortify the inner facade's effect; in addition to the 2 passive mental resistance and 3 active from fortify the inner facade, you're getting a third bonus health level and soaking three superficial damage per attack.<br /> <br /> Or, if you have daunt from presence 1 and buy melpominee at level 2, your new +2 to intimidation dice pools (and the resolve + composure roll to attack) no longer requires line of sight or being in your immediate vicinity -- <i>all</i> you need is to be within earshot. By default, you still must be able to see another to affect them with a targeted presence power, or vice versa in the case of non-targeted presence powers; otherwise, those targets are not targetable or susceptible to presence.<br /> <br /> That's why melpominee specifies working with awe, daunt, dread gaze, entrancement, or majesty. You can't entrance a vampire hidden by cloak of shadows, unseen presence, or vanish -- unless you have sense the unseen and have successfully pierced that obfuscate. With melpominee, you can as you no longer need to see them to target them -- you just need to know they're there. Even if the vampire is using mask of a thousand faces or imposter's guise to blend in with a crowd, without sense the unseen you're left targeting random mortals and hoping to get the right one; with melpominee, you can target them just by knowing they're somewhere in the crowd (but they still need to be able to hear you).<br /> <br /> Sure, noise-canceling earbuds still hard counter it, but rudimentary and mundane hard counters to whole-ass disciplines have existed since the very first 1st edition player got the idea their character wears sunglasses at night.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> ...especially not when it doles out access to one of the key features of one clan...V5 started with the implicit design goal of allowing somewhat open access to signature clan powers... </div> </div> </div>And, with the <i>explicit</i> design goal of eliminating clans as pseudo-classes. What you identify as &quot;key features&quot; of one clan or another simply no longer exist, and that's by design to grant players more freedom within the constraint of a single clan, or to play a given character archetype as one of several different clans. Even blood sorcery was reduced to three discipline sub-trees (counting koldunism now that Blood Sigils is out), and given as a discipline inherent to two clans who can pick and choose from those sub-trees as they see fit.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Chimerstry and Fata Morgana aren't necessary for Dementation 1-5 at all: the Dementation amalgam power itself already covers the encroaching delusions and loss of control aspects of the Discipline. </div> </div> </div>That only holds true if you view dementation exclusively as a vehicle for damaging willpower, and forcing derangements (now compulsions) on targets. Otherwise, that could not possibly be further from the truth: dementation level 1 is about inflaming or suppressing emotions, level 2 literally forces hallucinations on a target, and level 3 grants visions. What I described <i>is</i> the analog to older-edition dementation: presence is there to replicate passion (not just to meet chimerstry requisites), chimerstry and fata morgana <i>are</i> the analog to haunting, and active-use premonition is literally just eyes of chaos.<br /> <br /> Except, the v5 analog is a hell of a lot more broadly applicable, brings greater utility, and has a lot more control than a single discipline leveled to 5.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I hope we can at least agree that there's a vast, vast gulf between &quot;doing everything at once&quot; and &quot;doing one thing per Discipline level ever&quot;, but even if not, surely you can see how the latter setup strongly encourages pragmatic Discipline power selections over thematic ones, including selections based on clan themes.<br /> <br /> I still don't have a problem with making choices in and of itself. However, that doesn't mean that I have to accept all choice setups as desirable or fine as-is, and I don't accept that the choice between neat/evocative powers (if one happens to find the various Serpentis ports to be so) and optimal powers has to be as skewed as it is here. </div> </div> </div>Again, you're missing the point. Clans and characters in v5, by design, are no longer defined by a single discipline or powers within that discipline. You build based on the <i>totality</i> of discipline powers available, to create a single, holistic theme. Choices that are suboptimal or not pragmatic in the white room, can and do have strong uses within that theme -- it just depends what the theme is, and how well individual powers work within that remit.<br /> <br /> Here, I'll give you an example of a nagaraja NPC I just made for the chronicle I'm about to run.<br /> <br /> She's a trapdoor -- she havens in a famous haunted location and feeds from investigators who go there, and her bonus discipline dot is obfuscate (and I gave her the two-dot nagaraja merit, among others). She's not a dick to wraiths -- in fact one of her convictions is &quot;never be cruel to ghosts&quot; -- and her haven is also a shallowing, so the wraiths come to her rather than the other way round. But at the same time, she's there to preserve the Masquerade, but keep the shallowing strong while protecting it from mortals and other vampires, and that means walking a razor-thin line between letting a steady stream of &quot;evidence&quot; out while keeping anything revelatory in.<br /> <br /> So, with all that in mind I gave her sense the unseen, cloud memory, dementation, oblivion sight, where the veil thins, aura of decay, silence of death, and unseen passage. As she's not a dick to wraiths she doesn't need &quot;be a dick to wraiths&quot; ceremonies, which frees up her oblivion 1 spot from binding fetter and let me give her oblivion sight. Ashes to ashes might have been a good pick, but she has a better way to dispose of corpses being a nagaraja and all; so instead, she has two ways to see ghosts, a way to see other supernatural fuckery, and darkvision.<br /> <br /> Where the veil thins is kind of a weak pick for this character's purposes other than wraith warding, but it's wraith warding. Must-have. Aura of decay and host spirit are her trump card: if and when she needs a skill set other than her own she has wraith allies and retainers to ride shotgun. Again, recall she is <i>not</i> a dick to wraiths, and is the one protecting the shallowing and keeping the pathos flowing, which means in turn wraiths have little reason to screw her over and every reason to <i>help</i>.<br /> <br /> Obfuscate is still obfuscate, except hers works against wraiths thanks to her merit, and that allows her to sneaky-sneak when spectres are involved until such a time she's prepared to deal with it...which plays into her <i>own</i> skill set, which is focused on investigation, research, knowledge, forensics, and deduction. If she genuinely needs to know what a fetter may be, she's more than capable of doing it the old fashioned way. As it is, silence of death and unseen presence lets her sneak around investigators and do all the requisite &quot;unexplained noises&quot;, &quot;EMF interference&quot;, and &quot;items moving and lights going on and off&quot; that constitutes most paranormal &quot;evidence&quot;.<br /> <br /> Other than that, when it comes time for her to get a new discipline dot as the chronicle progresses (I like doing that to keep players on their toes), ghost in the machine is definitely next up as how it interferes with recording technology.<br /> <br /> Cloud memory and dementation are important to her, to help maintain the Masquerade while having a shallowing as a haven. If an investigator learns a little too much or finds something they shouldn't, a little amnesia goes a long way while feeding the location's mystique. Failing that, the occasional investigator going stark raving mad perpetuates the location's rep while discrediting anything said about it, and as a side benefit is a nice tasty treat for the ghosties.<br /> <br /> In other words, her entire discipline spread is based around a singular, holistic, theme: running a fake haunted house but making it look real, to hide the fact it <i>really is</i> haunted. And by coincidence it makes her a really good sheriff, so within Kindred society that's what she is.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":431,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/431-theodrim","user":"Theodrim","date":". 01-05-2024, 09:34 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4e","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/431-theodrim","name":"Theodrim","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar431_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1841"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-01-05T19:44:36-08:00","id":1528080,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>trueann</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1527926-ministry-s-discipline#post1527926\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Hi, guys! I'm wondering, what do you prefer: Serpentis or V5 Protean variant?</div> </div> </div> </div>Overall I prefer the older edition of Serpentis to the V5 Protean variant, but allowing Ministry members to pick up Chimerstry is a huge boon and, I think, puts Ministry members in a better position in V5 than they were in the prior editions.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/741-anubisxy","name":"AnubisXy","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar741_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5693"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-01-06T09:58:41-08:00","id":1528112,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Theodrim</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1527926-ministry-s-discipline?p=1528073#post1528073\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">It's not a &quot;completely new&quot; discipline, if the player already knows it. This is what the language means: to learn a completely new discipline, is to buy the first dot.</div> </div> </div> </div>A Discipline would be completely new to a player up to the point of gaining the first dot in it, even during character creation, but there is further verbiage on that page that makes me inclined to concede the point. I had been basing my position on page 231's mention of Vitae requirements to learn non-clan Disciplines in conjunction with the fact that honing in on a predator type is clearly framed as a learning process despite removing the experience costs, but 244 also provides a clear elaboration on these rules and when it says &quot;to <i>later</i> learn a completely new Discipline&quot;, I won't try to pretend that there's a reasonable interpretation of &quot;later&quot; that encompasses character creation.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> What you're missing is when you increase a discipline level, you're not just getting a new power. The powers you already have now function at the new level. The order in which you buy them doesn't matter, save for prerequisites. </div> </div> </div>I'm not sure what part of outright referencing scaling benefits from Discipline powers in my earlier response suggests that I'm 'missing' this dynamic, and I even agree that the order in which they're purchased doesn't matter as far as the progression of scaling goes. This lack of difference is precisely the reason why there are, as I mentioned, obvious incentives to get them as early as possible while they're still relatively cheap as far as the experience costs are concerned. The mere fact that a level one power, for example, scales with each dot that you subsequently gain in the Discipline isn't the same as establishing a reasonable basis for cost/value-equivalence between it and a level three power; Dread Gaze is generally going to have a more dramatic impact than Daunt even if the latter is scaled to a +3, and there's no way these trends with higher level Discipline powers don't factor prominently into the rationale for progressively increasing the cost for dots in Disciplines.<br /> <br /> But even if I accepted the questionable premise that generalized scaling of powers per Discipline dot was a meaningful value-equalizer irrespective of the native levels of powers chosen at any point in a Discipline's progression, this premise still neglects to consider powers that don't scale at all. I'll throw out a few examples in case someone wonders whether this is a rare and insignificant outlier, and when I say they don't scale, I mean that they have no Discipline-based dice pools (ones marked with an asterisk have Attribute + Skill dice pools), static or no bonuses, and no other rules which change based on one's number of dots in the Discipline (including modifying other powers that do scale):<br /> <br /> Animal Succulence, Blink*, Bond Famulus*, Cat's Grace, Corrosive Vitae, Oblivion Sight, Rapid Reflexes, Seal the Beast's Maw, Shadow Cloak, Slavish Devotion, Touch of Oblivion, The Binding Fetter, The Eyes of Beasts, Traversal*, Uncanny Grip, Unerring Aim, and <i>every single Protean power in the core book</i>.<br /> <br /> You obviously can't count on scaling to pick up the slack in situations where no chosen powers scale or most don't, but I'd question the ability for it to do so even in more common situations where only two out of five of all chosen powers don't scale. Even a single non-scaling level one power purchased at higher levels like 3-5 (or 4-5 for a level two power) would seriously throw off the balance for scaling-based compensations given the dramatic cost differences. The only way to mitigate this is to deliberately limit powers available at certain levels and/or make them minor or niche enough that the value dips to be closer to lower-level powers, but, again, this creates conflicts with the impact/value expectation from higher level Discipline powers, whether from players or generalized design rationales for their costs.<br /> <br /> I'll take your word for it where the general utility of Melpominee is concerned, but that still doesn't make it a necessary purchase to functionally replicate levels 1-5 to Dementation; at best it's an added perk while chasing after higher bonuses from Daunt, but Specialties, Merits, and Loresheet Advantages, or simply increasing Intimidation itself, are much cheaper ways to boost Intimidation and no amount of Presence after the first dot does anything to change the Difficulty of the secondary effect from Daunt.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> And, with the <i>explicit</i> design goal of eliminating clans as pseudo-classes. What you identify as &quot;key features&quot; of one clan or another simply no longer exist, and that's by design to grant players more freedom within the constraint of a single clan, or to play a given character archetype as one of several different clans. Even blood sorcery was reduced to three discipline sub-trees (counting koldunism now that Blood Sigils is out), and given as a discipline inherent to two clans who can pick and choose from those sub-trees as they see fit. </div> </div> </div>Unless I interpret this response as coming from a position that <i>any</i> affinities and challenges in accessing Disciplines or other perks based on clan is some sort of regression into the bad old days of clans as class equivalents, this reads as a non-sequitur - or perhaps even an attempt to resort to false dichotomies rather than engage with what I've actually said, but I'll give you the benefit of the doubt.<br /> <br /> If that is your position, then even setting aside my obvious disagreement, I'd have to have to wonder about what the various ways in which other design decisions - Clan Disciplines, amalgam powers that aren't as easy to access to Chimerstry, clan-based Merits and Loresheets, and arguably Clan Banes/Compulsions - undercut the achievement of this goal throughout the history of this edition say about the designer's commitment to it. After all, these and other clan-specific factors incentivize certain playstyles and introduce significant added challenges/costs to others, much like a 'pseudo-class' would.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> That only holds true if you view dementation exclusively as a vehicle for damaging willpower, and forcing derangements (now compulsions) on targets. Otherwise, that could not possibly be further from the truth: dementation level 1 is about inflaming or suppressing emotions, level 2 literally forces hallucinations on a target, and level 3 grants visions. What I described <i>is</i> the analog to older-edition dementation: presence is there to replicate passion (not just to meet chimerstry requisites), chimerstry and fata morgana <i>are</i> the analog to haunting, and active-use premonition is literally just eyes of chaos. </div> </div> </div>This only makes sense as a counter to what I've said if you think that I said the Dementation amalgam covers every single aspect of Dementation, which is a very strange way to read: <i>&quot;Chimerstry and Fata Morgana aren't necessary for Dementation 1-5 at all: the Dementation amalgam power itself already covers the encroaching delusions and loss of control aspects of the Discipline.&quot;</i><br /> <br /> I guess I'll break it down in case there's any lingering confusion: Chimerstry and Fata Morgana aren't necessary for Dementation 1-5 at all, because Dementation as an amalgam already covers particular aspects of the older Dementation Discipline that pertain to breaks from reality as an explanation for its effects (not the only explanation, but certainly one of them). This is <i>not</i> me saying that I think <i>every other power</i> that you mentioned can be ignored if one wishes to replicate the old Discipline's characteristics to any reasonably comprehensive extent. At most, I might suggest that Dementation can also cover the same conceptual ground as Passion insofar as Compulsions represent a range of intensified emotions - by that standard, Daunt's not <i>necessary</i> as a stand-in for Passion, or arguably even sufficient given that the controlled intensification of fear is a far cry from the uncontrolled fluctuations in emotion that Passion represents, but it's a painless addition.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Again, you're missing the point. Clans and characters in v5, by design, are no longer defined by a single discipline or powers within that discipline. </div> </div> </div>This is basically a retread of your earlier confusion over my position and I addressed it above, but I think there's some value in introducing a distinction here: clans and players aren't <i>entirely</i> defined by Disciplines, not that they ever were in prior editions either, but signature supernatural powers and affinities that they have, whether in the form of particular Discipline amagalms, Loresheet perks, or clan-specific Merits, play a notable part in their identity. This remains true in V5 by design as well, and fully opening up Chimerstry to the Ministry with no strings attached undercuts this, leading to my mixed feelings about it.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> You build based on the <i>totality</i> of discipline powers available, to create a single, holistic theme. Choices that are suboptimal or not pragmatic in the white room, can and do have strong uses within that theme -- it just depends what the theme is, and how well individual powers work within that remit. </div> </div> </div>I don't doubt that players and NPCs can have standout moments with particular Discipline powers even if they're less powerful or versatile than others, but that's not an effective counter to my position that the limits on Discipline choice in conjunction with substantial differences between powers where general efficacy is concerned strongly incentivize choosing the more broadly useful and effective powers over others, and thus less functional distinction between clans/characters who share these Discipline (which is my criterion for regarding the Ministry's situation as being worse under this Discipline's setup). The only way that it would be is if these moments are so numerous, so substantial, and so applicable to various seemingly niche choices across the board that there is in fact no such incentive or it is diminished to the point that its consequences are insignificant.<br /> <br /> Your Nagaraja example doesn't suffice to establish that, even if it is a neat build with interesting applications.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":27894,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27894-paramecius","user":"Paramecius","date":". 01-06-2024, 01:04 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27894-paramecius","name":"Paramecius","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27894_1.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2023"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"29"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-01-06T12:51:24-08:00","id":1528120,"title":"","content":"I like Serpentis myself, but I'm sure I can come up with some cool Amalgam tricks that can mimic higher levels of the Discipline with Protean.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>&quot;Power that needs to advertise itself is already losing&quot; Grand Admiral Thrawn <br /> <br /> &quot;Coronation Starscream? This is bad comedy!&quot; Galvatron","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27361-lysander","name":"Lysander","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27361_11.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2022"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3523"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-01-06T20:08:45-08:00","id":1528140,"title":"","content":"Personally I also feel that the Setites/Ministry are to tied to Serpentis for me to dump that Discipline even if I dislike unique Disciplines in general. But I have no doubt that I could work with Protean as well. I haven't looked into the details of Protean of V5, or V5 at all, but some individual snake flavor similar to the Gangrel could be cool.<br /> <br /> As an example that this Setite gets a look of an anaconda, this one gets of a cobra, this third one of a different snake and so on. Possibly tied into both facts and mythology/folk lore about this specific kind of snake. Or run it as bloodlines of the Setite Clan based on different kinds of snakes or what else you can come up with.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/970-gurkhal","name":"Gurkhal","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"863"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-01-06T21:57:31-08:00","id":1528141,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Paramecius</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1527926-ministry-s-discipline?p=1528112#post1528112\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> I'm not sure what part of outright referencing scaling benefits from Discipline powers in my earlier response suggests that I'm 'missing' this dynamic...</div> </div> </div> </div>The core of your argument as I understand it, is that buying lower-level powers with new dots is cost-ineffective. Certain disciplines' strong scaling mechanics -- namely, presence and fortitude -- is an intentional design feature to offset this cost-ineffectiveness, along with levels at which powers are supplementary to (or override the mechanics of) those lower-level powers.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> ...Dread Gaze is generally going to have a more dramatic impact than Daunt even if the latter is scaled to a +3... </div> </div> </div>To a single target, once per round, at the cost of a rouse check, with a significantly higher risk profile as a character has to spend their turn using it, and that it's actively resisted based on a dice pool that trends high for supernaturals. Not to mention its use is a potential Masquerade breach. Dread gaze is actually kinda crap in v5, especially compared to past editions.<br /> <br /> Daunt affects everyone within range for the duration of the scene, is free to activate, has use in social scenes as well as combat, can be activated <i>before</i> combat making its risk profile minimal, and carries little to no Masquerade risk as it doesn't force the vampire using it to do anything overtly supernatural. Sure, the roll to attack the vampire has a low difficulty making it largely effective against gifted or deadly mortals and supernaturals, but it's a rider which effectively controls out weak and average mortals for the vampire's purposes.<br /> <br /> To hell with what the &quot;discipline characteristics&quot; section of the write-up says in these cases. Dread gaze forces a vampire to make an overt display of supernatural power and therefore perceptible to anyone with eyesight and/or a camera, daunt forces a roll to even attack the user in the first place.<br /> <br /> &quot;Higher-level&quot; doesn't mean &quot;always better&quot;. They're different powers with different effects, which means variable utility and effect. The only straight upgrade to daunt is majesty, but that's a level 5 power with a high cost to use, and is the biggest Masquerade-breaker in the entire discipline making it a highly contingent power. The subset of circumstances in which majesty would be preferable -- or necessary -- to use in place of daunt, is actually quite small.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> ...this premise still neglects to consider powers that don't scale at all...<br /> <br /> Animal Succulence, Blink*, Bond Famulus*, Cat's Grace, Corrosive Vitae, Oblivion Sight, Rapid Reflexes, Seal the Beast's Maw, Shadow Cloak, Slavish Devotion, Touch of Oblivion, The Binding Fetter, The Eyes of Beasts, Traversal*, Uncanny Grip, Unerring Aim, and <i>every single Protean power in the core book</i>. </div> </div> </div>Okay, so how many of those are passive versus active effects, with set uses? Exactly how many of those <i>need</i> to scale to function or stay mechanically viable at higher-level play, or even have effects that could be subject to scaling in the first place? How many of those confer dice pool bonuses as rider, opposed to primary, effects?<br /> <br /> One and only one of those powers you mentioned has utility that really needs scaling: animal succulence. It has reduced effect on a blood potency 4 vampire and zero effect on a blood potency 5 vampire.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> The only way to mitigate this is to deliberately limit powers available at certain levels and/or make them minor or niche enough that the value dips to be closer to lower-level powers, but, again, this creates conflicts with the impact/value expectation from higher level Discipline powers, whether from players or generalized design rationales for their costs. </div> </div> </div>It sounds to me like disciplines were balanced to operate on a spectrum between synergistic scaling powers, or individually-strong powers with distinct effects. Where presence would be on one end of that spectrum where over one-third of powers published to date are augmentations to multiple other powers, and protean the other where all three out of ten augmentation powers (vicissitude, shapechange, and one with the land) are direct upgrades to individual lower-level powers.<br /> <br /> The outliers being blood sorcery and oblivion, on account of ritual/ceremony mechanics. And in the latter's case, ceremony prerequisites are deliberately designed to be weaker than other, comparable, disciplines to balance out the power of ceremonies they unlock. <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> If that is your position, then even setting aside my obvious disagreement, I'd have to have to wonder about what the various ways in which other design decisions - Clan Disciplines, amalgam powers that aren't as easy to access to Chimerstry, clan-based Merits and Loresheets, and arguably Clan Banes/Compulsions... </div> </div> </div>Note the second sentence of that paragraph, particularly <i>&quot;...grant players more freedom within the constraint of a single clan, or to play a given character archetype as one of several different clans.</i>&quot; A single discipline power existing per level, and proprietary disciplines, did enforce a given way to play each clan with little to no room to play off-type or explore different ways to approach concepts within the remit of discipline spread. There's a reason the most popular clans tended to be either those with highly versatile discipline spreads which allow for playing multiple archetypes, or a single overpowered discipline that overshadows anything else. It happens to be the same reason extra/variant clan discipline merits and bloodlines were so popular (other than min-maxing): they gave players freedom for off-type play or alternate ways to approach clans' themes.<br /> <br /> Setites in particular had so little room to explore the very thing they were supposed to be good at, White Wolf had to publish entire proprietary merits and not one but <i>two</i> forms of blood sorcery complete with proprietary paths and rituals, while introducing lore to give them blood sorcery inherently (but at out-of-clan cost). In fact, I'll go so far as to say setites would have made a lot more sense from the beginning, had serpentis never existed and they had akhu instead. But lest we assume I'm picking on setites, White Wolf managed the exact same fuck-up with OCT and koldunic sorcery.<br /> <br /> Bloodline merits are entirely optional, and neither lock in or out character archetypes. The mere existence of variant banes and compulsions demonstrate perfectly how v5 moves away from enforcing character archetypes to clan. But more than anything else, predator type does more to give players freedom by way of allowing access to a fourth discipline of the player's choice at character generation, entirely contingent on how they want to play and how they've built their character.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> This only makes sense as a counter to what I've said if you think that I said the Dementation amalgam covers every single aspect of Dementation, which is a very strange way to read: <i>&quot;Chimerstry and Fata Morgana aren't necessary for Dementation 1-5 at all: the Dementation amalgam power itself already covers the encroaching delusions and loss of control aspects of the Discipline.&quot;</i> </div> </div> </div>You wrote what you wrote: the dominate 2 power of dementation covers every (game mechanical) aspect of dementation. Stripping it down to its nuts and bolts, that's what OG dementation does: it damages willpower by way of enforced derangements and virtue/frenzy checks at altered difficulties. You planted the goalpost, not me.<br /> <br /> If that's all you're looking at, which completely ignores haunting and eyes of chaos, that's all you're going to see. And how do I know that's all you're looking at?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I guess I'll break it down in case there's any lingering confusion: Chimerstry and Fata Morgana aren't necessary for Dementation 1-5 at all, because Dementation as an amalgam already covers particular aspects of the older Dementation Discipline that pertain to breaks from reality as an explanation for its effects (not the only explanation, but certainly one of them). </div> </div> </div>This is how I know that's all you're looking at. The discipline power that doesn't cause targets to hallucinate because hallucination is neither mechanism of action nor stated effect, is sufficient to replicate the dementation power that specifically causes hallucination, but the two discipline powers that actually do cause targets to hallucinate isn't necessary? The only way that makes sense, is for you to be looking exclusively at dementation's willpower-attacking mechanisms.<br /> <br /> Not to put too fine a point on this, but v5 chimerstry has haunting's effect (two-dice penalty on next action after hallucinating), just to make the connective tissue between the disciplines clearer.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> This is <i>not</i> me saying that I think <i>every other power</i> that you mentioned can be ignored if one wishes to replicate the old Discipline's characteristics to any reasonably comprehensive extent. </div> </div> </div>That's exactly what you wrote. I don't think you realized the implications of what you wrote, when you did.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> This is basically a retread of your earlier confusion over my position and I addressed it above, but I think there's some value in introducing a distinction here: clans and players aren't <i>entirely</i> defined by Disciplines... </div> </div> </div>Yes they were, and I'm more than happy to give you a counterexample in addition to my previous remarks about setites and akhu.<br /> <br /> In game fluff and as described by published text, the tankiest clan in the world of darkness are the ventrue. Even if you get past their resources, status, and allies, you have to deal with their retainers. And even if you get past them, ventrue are supposed to be phenomenally tough and able to shrug off blows that would send any other vampire to torpor or final death. They didn't conquer and rule the majority of Europe for millennia against the likes of brujah and tzimisce, weather the inquisition and anarch revolt, and go on to form and lead the camarilla, because they were easy to kill.<br /> <br /> Except they are. To wit, they're the flimsiest clan in any iteration of the camarilla across any edition until v5, despite in-clan fortitude. If past-edition VtM had any sense of ludonarrative harmony, the road to toreador and tremere duopoly over the camarilla would have been paved with ventrue corpses. Conditioned mortal security and ghouls are a joke at best, at worst a free fill-up on the assailant's way to whack the ventrue; presence is easy to overcome, and fortitude is the worst discipline in the game.<br /> <br /> Every other camarilla clan save the gangrel, has auspex to know when and if shit's about to go down, obfuscate to &quot;not be there&quot; when it does, or celerity to get the hell out of Dodge. Toreador and malkavians even have two of those three. The gangrel get fortitude, earth meld to hide, and animal form to fight back or escape.<br /> <br /> There is absolutely no way to play a ventrue who's tanky in any sense of the word, without house rules, extra clan discipline, going out-of-clan, or being antitribu. And that's the one thing -- the <i>one, single, solitary thing</i> -- at which ventrue are supposed to be exceptional. I've seen it time and again across over twenty years of playing VtM/VtDA, it's impossible to play a ventrue in a game consistent to how they are represented in lore, and expect that character to survive longer than a story or two. It's such a joke, both Hardestadts getting whacked by Tyler and Theo Bell are lampshade-hanging moments, on account of how hard ventrue are supposed to be able to kill in fluff versus how easy they are to kill in actual play.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4e","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/431-theodrim","name":"Theodrim","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar431_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1841"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-01-07T13:32:15-08:00","id":1528164,"title":"","content":"If were talking v5 I'd actually argue protean is a bad match for the ministry. Serpantis works for the setites as a nod to Conan and as motif but protean doesn't really click with the clan of faith-theirs no real thematic or ecological link. If anything if you're going ministry I actually think they should either have blood sorcery, oblivion with a load of snakey rituals thrown in or auspex as a mystic discipline.<br /> <br /> Plus if protean is getting to be another thaumaturgy witn 3 disciplines clumsily cramed in and since the fandom doesn't even seem to like the discipline from the two threads I've seen on it....","signature":"","edited":{"userid":26986,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26986-ragged-robin","user":"Ragged Robin","date":". 01-07-2024, 04:36 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26986-ragged-robin","name":"Ragged Robin","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26986_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1720"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-01-07T13:35:03-08:00","id":1528165,"title":"","content":"While I understand why people associate Serpentis with Protean, I am partial to interpreting it more as a form of Blood Magic. A friend of mine explained to me once that the fifth rank was clearly based on Egyptian magic, and that got me thinking along those lines. Its higher level powers of corruption clearly have nothing to do with shapechanging. So rather than define it by its shapechanging powers and ignore everything else, I just think it makes better sense to interpret them as also being part of this &quot;magical tradition&quot;. Serpentis just seems to have been powers thrown together in order to create Thoth Amon (Thulsa Doom in the 1982 movie).<br /> <br /> So in my chronicles, I just made Blood Magic one of their in-clan disciplines, gave the name &quot;Path of the Wyrm's Blessing&quot; as the main path (Serpentis), and assigned them several other existing Paths as part of the magical canon they could learn - Path of Corruption from the Players Guide (it works better for Setites than for Tremere, though I make it known to secret Order of the Wyrm within the Tremere) plus all the other Paths called Akhu created for the sourcebook Blood Magic: Secrets of Thaumaturgy. Once the various Akhu paths were added, it cemented the idea to just treat the Serpentis powers as a Blood Magic Path.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-01-07T15:16:20-08:00","id":1528178,"title":"","content":"<a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/431-theodrim\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar431_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"431\" data-vbnamecard=\"431\">Theodrim</a><br /> <br /> The scope of our discussion has veered from the subject of this thread to the point that I think it should leave it behind for a <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1528172-v5-discipline-debate\">different one</a>.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27894-paramecius","name":"Paramecius","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27894_1.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2023"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"29"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-01-08T16:26:17-08:00","id":1528231,"title":"","content":"Soooooooo, what I learned from this discussion is: keep Requiem rolling, write a VtM book, and let's forget V5. And, yeah, I liked a lot of the old Serpentis, and VDA20 is indeed sweet. I've always been a edgelady, anyways, and Setites and Lasombra are usually my clans of choice.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27825-mrdrika","name":"MrDrika","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27825_1.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2023"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"64"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-01-08T16:27:20-08:00","id":1528232,"title":"","content":"Malkavians &amp; Followers Of Set myself with the Gangrel and Brujah close behind.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>&quot;Power that needs to advertise itself is already losing&quot; Grand Admiral Thrawn <br /> <br /> &quot;Coronation Starscream? This is bad comedy!&quot; Galvatron","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83e\udd70","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27361-lysander","name":"Lysander","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27361_11.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2022"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3523"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-01-29T04:37:23-08:00","id":1529637,"title":"","content":"Thematically, yeah, Egyptian magic makes more sense than Serpentis in any edition.<br /> <br /> If we're stuck with Serpentis, though, I'm in the crowd that thinks VTR2e Protean &gt; V20DA Serpentis &gt; V5 Protean &gt; older versions of Serpentis.<br /> <br /> V5 Protean would be better if a) they dropped the &quot;one power per dot&quot; rule and b) dropped the &quot;1XP per session&quot; rule. But Requiem 2e's Protean is just *chef's kiss*.<br /> <br /> If V6 based its Protean on that one, it would serve all the Protean-alikes really well. I'd probably loosen it somewhat for flavour -- so that level 5 could turn Setites into a sandstorm, Tlacique into black smoke, and Tzimisce into a pool of blood, for example.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Writer, publisher, performer<br /> Mostly he/his, sometimes she/her IRL <a href=\"https://adam-lowe.com\" target=\"_blank\">https://adam-lowe.com</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5291-adambeyoncelowe","name":"adambeyoncelowe","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5291_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2802"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}